4. Proposed Solution

Queueker resolves these issues by introducing an elegant, isolated queue management solution characterized by the following features:
* Domain-Specific Authentication & Isolation: Employs JWT-based security to ensure institutions log in independently. An active domain restricts queue creation to its specific scope.
* Real-time Synchronization: Integrates Socket.IO to sync queue progression instantaneously between administration dashboards and customer views.
* Dedicated Interfaces:
* Customer Portal (Customer.jsx): Allows users to intuitively browse active institutions and request tokens.
* Administrator Dashboard (Admin.jsx): Grants institutions the power to manage tokens (call next, complete, skip) and maintain overall queue metrics.
* Aesthetic & Responsive Design: Uses a sleek, modern UI with dynamic feedback mechanisms (like Toast.jsx alerts) and fluid route transitions to guarantee a premium user experience regardless of the device.



5. Implementation Tech Stack

Frontend Layer:
* React 19 & Vite: Ensures rapid development, hot module replacement, and optimal build performance.
* React Router DOM: Enables smooth, dynamic client-side pagination allowing seamless transitions between Home, Auth, Customer, and Admin modules.
* Socket.IO-Client: Implements low-latency, bidirectional WebSocket events for live queue monitoring.
* Vanilla CSS: Employs meticulously crafted responsive and modern styling, inclusive of premium visual features (e.g., glassmorphism, dynamic .Toast indicators).
Backend Layer:
* Node.js & Express: Forms the robust foundation for serving RESTful APIs (/api/auth, /api/queues, /api/tokens).
* Socket.IO Server: Powers the real-time event broadcasting to multiple clients seamlessly.
* Authentication: Utilizes bcryptjs for strong password hashing and jsonwebtoken (JWT) for stateless, securitized route protection.
* Database Integration: MongoDB is orchestrated through Mongoose, mapping strictly defined relational schemas tailored to domain specifics.








